# Two-anchor geometry and fusion.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR
	bool "Two-anchor door geometry and inside/outside fusion"
	help
	  Door swing angle from the frame-to-leaf UWB distance (ultrawidelock_door.h), and
	  the side-of-door verdict plus triangle-inequality consistency gate over
	  two anchors' distances to one phone (ultrawidelock_fusion.h).

	  Integer arithmetic over caller-owned structs: no allocation, no threads,
	  no static state, and no platform dependency. The whole module is about
	  1 KB of flash and zero bytes of RAM until a caller declares a struct.

	  Default n, and nothing selects it yet. The logic was written and
	  host-tested ahead of the hardware it describes, so turning it on is a
	  separate decision that comes with a measured RAM budget -- the
	  DWM3001CDK Matter image has roughly 6 KB free and a new static
	  allocation there is a decision rather than a detail.

	  Also carries ultrawidelock_side.h: the fail-closed INSIDE/OUTSIDE/THRESHOLD/
	  UNKNOWN gate for passive approach unlock. That gate is separate from
	  ultrawidelock_fusion_may_predict() and must be selected explicitly via
	  CONFIG_ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE before it can suppress a passive unlock.

	  Both halves carry a MOUNTING requirement that no code can substitute
	  for: the two anchors must sit equidistant from the hinge (ultrawidelock_door),
	  and symmetrically about the door plane (ultrawidelock_fusion). Read those headers
	  before drilling anything.

if ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	bool "Fail-closed side gate for passive approach unlock"
	default n
	help
	  When y, UNLOCK_PREDICT and UNLOCK_THRESHOLD from ultrawidelock_approach are
	  released only if ultrawidelock_side_may_passive_unlock() is true: side must be
	  confident OUTSIDE with fresh evidence, quorum, and no contradiction.

	  UNKNOWN, INSIDE, THRESHOLD, stale, replayed, multi-phone, and
	  degraded evidence all suppress passive unlock. NFC Express Mode,
	  Apple Home commands, and mechanical operation are not gated here.

	  Default n preserves today's behaviour. Turning this on without
	  commissioned BLE witnesses will refuse every passive unlock -- that
	  is intentional fail-closed behaviour, not a regression.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_PEER_EMIT
	bool "Bench: log the credential peer address at L2CAP CoC open"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	default n
	help
	  Logs `SIDE peer=<AdvA> type=<public|random>` when the credential L2CAP CoC
	  opens and `SIDE peer=clear` when it closes, so bench tooling can push
	  the phone's current address to the BLE witnesses as an advertising
	  filter. Without it a witness summarises every advertiser in the room.

	  That address is personal data. A resolvable private address identifies
	  whoever is standing at the door for as long as it lasts, so this is
	  default n and must stay off in a shipped image. It is a bench aid, not
	  a product feature: the witnesses need a way to learn which advertiser
	  is the credential, and this is the temporary one.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_FEED_RTT
	bool "Bench: ingest SF1 witness summaries over SEGGER RTT down-buffer"
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE
	depends on USE_SEGGER_RTT
	default n
	help
	  Parses ASCII lines:
	    SF1 in=<dbm> out=<dbm> th=<dbm> ni=<n> no=<n> nt=<n>
	  from RTT down-buffer 0 into ultrawidelock_side_filter_feed().

	  Bench only: it needs a debug probe holding the RTT connection, so it is
	  not a path a deployed lock can use. It used to default y, which meant
	  selecting the gate silently selected a J-Link dependency. Default n now,
	  and the SIDE overlay turns it on explicitly.

	  This is currently the ONLY transport into the gate. Until a witness
	  link over UART or Matter exists, ULTRAWIDELOCK_SIDE_GATE without this option
	  refuses every passive unlock, exactly as its own help warns.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_BASELINE_MM
	int "Distance between the two anchors, millimetres"
	default 1200
	range 100 10000
	help
	  Measured centre-to-centre between the two anchor antennas. This is the
	  one side of the phone-anchor-anchor triangle that is known, so every
	  consistency check is relative to it and a wrong value here silently
	  mis-sizes all of them.

	  Longer is better for discrimination: the dead band is a fixed number of
	  millimetres, so a longer baseline spends a smaller fraction of the
	  approach on "ambiguous". If more than 20% of approaches land in the
	  dead band, move the anchors further apart rather than shrinking the
	  band.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_TOL_MM
	int "Triangle-inequality tolerance, millimetres"
	default 90
	range 0 1000
	help
	  Slack on both triangle tests, sized at roughly 3 sigma of the ranging
	  jitter so ordinary noise at the geometric boundary does not read as an
	  attack. 90 assumes the 30 mm jitter stage A targets; re-derive it from
	  the jitter actually measured, because a tolerance smaller than the
	  noise rejects real approaches.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DEADBAND_MM
	int "Ambiguity dead band, millimetres"
	default 60
	range 0 1000
	help
	  Distance-difference smaller than this is called UNKNOWN rather than a
	  side, because the sign is inside the noise. Roughly 2 sigma of ranging
	  jitter. A phone in the doorway is genuinely ambiguous and has to read
	  that way; UNKNOWN permits prediction, so this is a bias toward the door
	  opening.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_STALE_MS
	int "Satellite report lifetime, milliseconds"
	default 1500
	range 100 30000
	help
	  A report older than this is discarded and the verdict falls back to
	  UNKNOWN, which permits prediction. Geometry from a stale distance is
	  worse than no geometry because it looks authoritative.

	  Size it above the satellite's worst-case report interval plus its link
	  latency, or a healthy satellite will keep expiring between reports.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SELF_INSIDE
	bool "This node is the INSIDE anchor"
	default y
	help
	  Which side of the door THIS board is mounted on. A mounting fact, not a
	  code fact, and it has to be written down somewhere -- getting it
	  backwards inverts the verdict, so the node would withhold prediction
	  from people inside the house and grant it to people outside.

	  Default y because the door node carries the lock and the credential,
	  and that belongs on the protected side. Set n only if this specific
	  board is physically the outer anchor.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM
	bool "LIS2DH12 impact and tamper interrupt"
	depends on I2C && GPIO
	help
	  Configure the accelerometer's INT1 generator over I2C and latch its
	  edges from a GPIO callback, so the existing 250 ms loop can ask whether
	  the door was struck.

	  Does NOT use Zephyr's lis2dh sensor driver, which is already linked
	  into both shipping CDK images and stays dead. That is a RAM decision
	  rather than a preference: CONFIG_LIS2DH_TRIGGER_GLOBAL_THREAD puts an
	  ISR bottom half on k_sys_work_q, measured at 3,568 B of its 4,096 B
	  during a live unlock, and CONFIG_LIS2DH_TRIGGER_OWN_THREAD costs a
	  1,024 B stack. This path costs one gpio_callback, one atomic_t and one
	  24 B classifier state, with no new thread and no new work queue.

	  Needs a board whose devicetree has an accel0 alias with an irq-gpios
	  property. The DWM3001CDK has both.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM_THRESHOLD_MG
	int "Impact threshold, milli-g"
	default 2000
	range 32 4000
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM
	help
	  Transient acceleration that counts as a strike, measured after the
	  high-pass filter so gravity is not part of the budget. Quantised to
	  32 mg steps by the +/-4 g full scale the driver selects, and clamped
	  to the register's 7-bit range.

	  2000 is a PLACEHOLDER, not a recommendation. The number that matters is
	  "louder than closing this door normally", which is a property of the
	  door, its hinges and where the board is screwed. Size it from a bench
	  capture of 20 normal closes and 20 slams; if the two populations
	  overlap at every threshold, this approach is finished and the honest
	  move is to ship the angle states with no tamper signal.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM_LOG_LEVEL
	int "Log level for the impact driver"
	default 3
	range 0 4
	depends on ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_SLAM
	help
	  Init-time only. Nothing on the interrupt path logs, and nothing can:
	  the callback runs in interrupt context beside a ranging arm deadline of
	  roughly 1836 us.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DOOR_HINGE_FRAME_MM
	int "Hinge axis to the frame anchor, millimetres"
	default 800
	range 1 5000
	help
	  Side a of the triangle ultrawidelock_door.h solves. A PLACEHOLDER, like
	  the baseline beside it in overlay-anchor.conf: the number that matters
	  is where the two boards actually ended up on this door, which is a
	  tape-measure fact and not a default anyone can ship.

	  Wrong here, every reported angle is biased and the ajar alarm fires at
	  the wrong opening. The board says so once at startup only when the
	  geometry is impossible, not when it is merely wrong.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DOOR_HINGE_LEAF_MM
	int "Hinge axis to the leaf anchor, millimetres"
	default 800
	range 1 5000
	help
	  Side b of the same triangle, and the same PLACEHOLDER status as the
	  frame distance above.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_DOOR_OFFSET_MDDEG
	int "Angle the geometry reports with the door SHUT, millidegrees"
	default 0
	range 0 179999
	help
	  theta0: subtracted from every reading so a shut door reports 0.

	  A PLACEHOLDER at 0, which is what a perfectly aligned pair of anchors
	  would give and no real pair does. ultrawidelock_door.h says how to get
	  the real one: solve it from three measured points (shut, ~45, ~90
	  degrees) rather than measuring to the hinge pin, because the hinge axis
	  is not where it looks like it is.

config ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR_AJAR_DWELL_S
	int "How long the door may stand open, bolt thrown, before the ajar alarm"
	default 60
	range 1 3600
	help
	  A DoorAjar alarm (door-lock-cluster.xml AlarmCodeEnum, value 7) once
	  the leaf has been away from CLOSED for this long while the Matter node
	  still reports the bolt LOCKED.

	  60 is a PLACEHOLDER chosen the same way the impact threshold's 2000 mg
	  is not: nothing has measured how long this door is normally held open
	  during a walk-through. Long, deliberately -- the cost of guessing high
	  is a late alarm, and the cost of guessing low is an owner who learns to
	  ignore this event. Size it from the same bench session that sizes the
	  impact threshold.

endif # ULTRAWIDELOCK_ANCHOR
